A signal answers “what is the value now”, an Observable answers “what happened”.
For state you take a signal; for events and request cancellation, a stream.

```ts
const first = signal('Anna');
const last = signal('Ivanova');

let calls = 0;
const full = computed(() => {
  calls++;
  return `${first()} ${last()}`;
});

full(); // calls === 1
full(); // calls === 1 — the value is cached
first.set('Maria');
full(); // calls === 2 — recomputed on the first read after the change
first.set('Maria');
full(); // calls === 2 — the value is the same, dependants were not disturbed
```

Dependencies are collected dynamically: an `if` branch that was not entered does not
become a dependency.

```ts
@Component({ selector: 'app-theme', template: '' })
export class ThemeComponent {
  theme = signal<'dark' | 'light'>('dark');

  constructor() {
    effect(() => {
      localStorage.setItem('theme', this.theme()); // a side effect — appropriate
    });

    effect((onCleanup) => {
      const id = setInterval(poll, this.interval());
      onCleanup(() => clearInterval(id)); // on recomputation and on destruction
    });
