Writing to a signal from an `effect` is forbidden by default — which saves you from loops.

```ts
const query = signal('');
const page = signal(1);

const results = computed(() => {
  const q = query();
  const p = untracked(page); // read it, but do not restart when the page changes
  return search(q, p);
});

effect(() => {
  const value = this.form(); // this is what we subscribe to
  untracked(() => this.logger.log(value)); // the logger may read other signals —
  // they must not become dependencies of the effect
});
```

A common cause of “the effect fires one time too many” is a signal read inside a called
method: a dependency is recorded by the fact of the read, not by its position in the code.

```ts
const items = signal<string[]>([]);

items.set(['a']);                     // a whole new value
items.update((list) => [...list, 'b']); // derived from the current one

items().push('c');   // the reference did not change — nobody finds out

// the comparison is by reference, so for objects you create a new one
const user = signal({ name: 'Anna' });
user.update((u) => ({ ...u, name: 'Maria' }));

// the comparison rule can be your own
const coords = signal({ x: 0 }, { equal: (a, b) => a.x === b.x });
```

