`inject()` works only in an injection context: class fields, the constructor,
provider factories, guards and interceptors. Outside one there is `runInInjectionContext`.
An interface cannot be a token: types are erased at compile time.

```ts
// environment: root → route providers → ...
export const routes: Routes = [
  { path: 'admin', providers: [AdminService], loadChildren: () => import('./admin') },
];

// elements: an instance of its own per component
@Component({
  selector: 'app-tab',
  providers: [TabState], // every <app-tab> gets its own TabState
  template: '',
})
export class TabComponent {}
```

The lookup goes up the element tree to the root component, then into the tree of
environment injectors. Found nowhere means `NullInjectorError: No provider for ...`.

```ts
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class Analytics {} // injected nowhere — will not reach the bundle

// this way the service is always in the bundle, even when it is not needed
bootstrapApplication(App, { providers: [Analytics] });
```

There is one instance per application. If you need an instance per component,
the provider is declared on the component itself:

```ts
@Component({ providers: [Analytics], selector: 'app-x', template: '' })
export class XComponent {}
```

